The Operating Systems Mini Project is a project created by the students of Florida Polytechnic University for the COP-4610 Section 3 Operating Systems Concepts course taught by Dr. Luis Jaimes. The aim of the project is to use xv6 to accomplish the following goals:

1A.) Create a file that prints the number of times the function getpid() has been called. 
1B.) Create a file that prints the number of system calls the operating system has called.
1C.) Create a file that prints the number of successful system calls.
2A.) Convert the scheduler into a Multi Level Feedback Queue.
2B.) Create a file that prints the number of time slices a process has used up at each queue.

Running the Project:
To run the project, it is assumed that you have Ubuntu downloaded on your computer. If you do not, see the installing_instructions file.
1.) Run the SystemStart.bat file.
2.) Once the file has opened, run the command "make qemu-nox". This will compile and run xv6.
3.) From there, you can run commands in your xv6 environment. See the users/makefile.mk for list of valid commands.
4.) To exit xv6, quickly press ctrl+a, release, and press x on your keyboard.

Commands Associated with each Goal:
1A - getpid, PartA
1B - PartB
1C - PartC
2B - pstest, tick-test {ticks}

Note: This project was developed in a Windows 11 environment. Results on other operating systems may vary.